WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.110
Comprehensible compelling input
is fine, but it's something

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:05.790
where we need an assist.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:09.900
Hi there Steve Kaufmann here today
and I'm going to talk to you today

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:15.390
about CI, which can be compelling
input or comprehensible input.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:18.250
And I'm going to talk
about the limits of CI.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:23.040
Uh, remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:24.210
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:29.640
Uh, if you're on a podcast service,
uh, you know, please leave a review.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:30.420
I appreciate it.

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:34.710
So, uh, if you follow me on these,
on my YouTube channel, you'll know

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:39.780
that I'm a great believer in Stephen
Krashen's input hypothesis that

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:41.580
we learn languages through input.

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:47.850
And that ideally that input should
be, and input means essentially

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:54.495
listening and reading, um, that that
input should be comprehensible and

00:00:54.495 --> 00:00:57.074
meaningful and ideally compelling.

00:00:57.675 --> 00:01:03.074
And so this then is the term CI and
there's a whole range of teaching

00:01:03.074 --> 00:01:09.345
activities that have developed around
this concept of creating CI, compelling or

00:01:09.345 --> 00:01:14.535
comprehensible, of course, for input to be
compelling, it has to be comprehensible.

00:01:14.955 --> 00:01:17.445
It can be comprehensible and boring.

00:01:18.024 --> 00:01:22.440
But it can't be, it's difficult for it
to be compelling and not comprehensible.

00:01:23.039 --> 00:01:27.060
Uh, although it can be of great interest,
but you don't fully understand it.

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:30.240
So to that extent, it's semi
comprehensive, but very compelling.

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:35.820
But at any rate CI, ingesting
comprehensible, compelling

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:39.570
input, meaningful messaging
as Krashen refers to it.

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:45.929
The more of this quantity of meaningful
messages we get the more our brain

00:01:45.929 --> 00:01:50.639
starts to become familiar with
the language and we acquire words

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:53.160
and familiarity with the language.

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:55.560
And eventually we end up being
able to speak the language.

00:01:56.580 --> 00:02:01.649
So I do believe in the power of
CI and I think these methods, new

00:02:01.649 --> 00:02:05.190
methods of language instruction
built around CI are good, but I

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:07.740
also feel there are certain limits.

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:13.350
First of all, when we start in a new
language, nothing is comprehensible

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:16.230
because you don't know the
language, you're starting at zero.

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:20.910
Obviously if you're going from
Spanish to Portuguese, some

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:22.470
things are comprehensible.

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:26.609
At least when you read, even as an
English speaker, if we see something

00:02:26.609 --> 00:02:30.030
in French or Spanish, there are
words there that we recognize.

00:02:31.109 --> 00:02:35.770
So some things are comprehensible but
generally speaking, especially if we're

00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:37.530
listening to it, it's not comprehensible.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:43.110
So to start off, you know, right
at the beginning, you know, we can

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:44.640
start with comprehensible input.

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:51.640
So we have to have some way of
getting, you know, stepping ourselves

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:55.800
up, empowering ourselves up to a
point where we are actually able

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:57.780
to deal with comprehensible input.

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:01.670
As I've said, that's where I
use the mini stories at LingQ.

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:02.760
Because the...

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:05.640
and I like the idea of
a lot of repetition.

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:12.450
I have a high degree of tolerance
for repetition of relatively

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:17.400
uninteresting content, because I'm
very motivated at first to at least

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.110
get a toehold in the language.

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:23.520
After a while, I get tired of this,
but initially I'm quite capable of

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:28.290
subjecting myself to, uh, repetitive
content, repetitive listening, repetitive

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:32.984
reading, looking up words, uh, in
order to get a toehold to the language.

00:03:33.705 --> 00:03:38.775
The second thing about comprehensible
input, and I've read now quite a bit on

00:03:38.775 --> 00:03:44.025
the subject, people like Paul Nation in
New Zealand and others, uh, there's a lot

00:03:44.025 --> 00:03:45.735
of raw material available on the internet.

00:03:45.735 --> 00:03:49.815
If you search comprehensible
input, extensive reading, uh,

00:03:49.825 --> 00:03:51.975
you can find lots of information.

00:03:52.485 --> 00:03:57.645
And one of the sort of traditional
approaches to this idea of, of using

00:03:57.645 --> 00:04:03.885
input to acquire the language is to
say that using the example of the

00:04:03.885 --> 00:04:09.855
native speaker who apparently learns
a thousand words a year and the native

00:04:09.855 --> 00:04:13.635
speaker learns a thousand words a year
because the native speaker has so much

00:04:13.635 --> 00:04:18.704
exposure to his or her own language,
whether it be listening or reading.

00:04:19.305 --> 00:04:23.835
And so the native speaker doesn't need
to look words up the native speaker can

00:04:23.835 --> 00:04:28.665
infer meaning, can guess at meaning,
gets these words in so many different

00:04:28.665 --> 00:04:33.570
contexts that they simply become
part of the sort of active vocabulary

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:37.799
words and phrases, expressions, new
expressions, and use slang words.

00:04:37.979 --> 00:04:39.180
They hear them on TV.

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:42.479
They read them here or there, and
that becomes part of their vocabulary.

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:47.520
Um, for someone learning a foreign
language, it's more difficult.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:55.359
So, uh, there was one paper I read, which
talked about maximum 2% unknown words.

00:04:55.664 --> 00:05:01.755
So that's one in 50 unknown words, that's
kind of the level you need to be at so

00:05:01.755 --> 00:05:07.905
that you can sort of naturally learn these
words by guessing, or by encountering

00:05:07.905 --> 00:05:11.294
these words in different contexts and
gradually getting a sense of what they

00:05:11.294 --> 00:05:16.395
mean, you know, to acquire words that way.

00:05:17.115 --> 00:05:21.195
Uh, it would be tremendously
time-consuming because as I've said,

00:05:21.195 --> 00:05:23.354
many times frequency drops off.

00:05:24.539 --> 00:05:29.130
So for the first thousand words
where there are a lot of what they

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:35.250
call function words like, uh, you
know, "the", "and", uh, "by", "with",

00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:38.909
"from", "I", "you", "he": common verbs.

00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:41.159
That's all there in the first thousand.

00:05:41.190 --> 00:05:43.440
And we get those so often
that we learned them.

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:49.080
There it is possible to acquire them
through, acquire them through context

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:51.599
in, in more or less comprehensive input.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:55.844
It's not, in my opinion, if you're
dealing with a foreign language, if

00:05:55.844 --> 00:05:59.895
I were to start on, Finnish on LingQ,
I would want to look up every word,

00:05:59.985 --> 00:06:05.145
I want to know which word is "by"
or "with", or "because", and I'll

00:06:05.145 --> 00:06:07.215
forget it, but I'll look it up again.

00:06:07.695 --> 00:06:10.515
And that way that speeds me along.

00:06:10.575 --> 00:06:14.865
Otherwise I will just be looking at the
string of five words and I don't, I don't

00:06:14.865 --> 00:06:19.565
know which one is the verb, which one is
"by", "with", "because", "from", "and".

00:06:20.385 --> 00:06:25.620
I look them up and eventually I
remember what I looked up, not

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:27.129
at first, but I do eventually.

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:29.489
And, but never...

00:06:29.549 --> 00:06:31.680
in any case, the first
thousand words are easier.

00:06:31.919 --> 00:06:34.500
The second thousand words,
we're now dealing with words

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:35.789
that don't show up that often.

00:06:36.719 --> 00:06:40.200
Where you would have to, because
again, theoretically, you need to

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:44.880
meet a word eight times, according
to some research in order to have a

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:48.720
50% chance of remembering that word.

00:06:49.215 --> 00:06:52.005
Uh, words that don't show up
very often that might show up

00:06:52.035 --> 00:06:53.535
five times in a whole book.

00:06:54.195 --> 00:06:58.935
Uh, you've got to read a lot of
books in order to meet that word

00:06:58.965 --> 00:07:01.125
often enough to remember it.

00:07:01.155 --> 00:07:06.855
And particularly if you're relying on, you
know, inferring or guessing the meaning

00:07:06.855 --> 00:07:12.195
of that word, if you're able to look it
up, if as at LingQ you have differential,

00:07:12.315 --> 00:07:17.625
you know, uh, highlighting to remind
you that you've seen the word before.

00:07:17.895 --> 00:07:22.515
If you have, you know, lists that you
can review after you finish a lesson,

00:07:22.784 --> 00:07:27.195
all of these different things can help
you, you know, acquire these words

00:07:27.224 --> 00:07:33.585
more quickly so that you don't need
to rely on being able to read through

00:07:33.765 --> 00:07:37.544
material that is so easy for you
that there's only 2% unknown words.

00:07:37.635 --> 00:07:40.335
You actually can read material
that's more difficult.

00:07:40.950 --> 00:07:45.599
Uh, and that way by using something
like LingQ, or if, even if you're

00:07:45.599 --> 00:07:48.750
reading on your Kindle and you look
up words in a dictionary or whatever

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:54.480
it might be, uh, you're able to deal
with more than 2% unknown words.

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:57.780
And every time you look the word
up, you tuck it away somewhere.

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:00.120
You forget it, but you're
working your way through.

00:08:00.730 --> 00:08:03.870
You're making that input comprehensible.

00:08:04.289 --> 00:08:07.440
You're not deliberately trying
to hang on to those words.

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:12.974
You're making the input comprehensible
and in time, those words as you meet

00:08:12.974 --> 00:08:17.205
them again and again, are going to
become a part of your vocabulary.

00:08:17.234 --> 00:08:22.724
So again, comprehensive or
compelling input is fine, but it's

00:08:22.724 --> 00:08:24.794
something where we need an assist.

00:08:25.094 --> 00:08:27.435
Another assist is audio.

00:08:27.794 --> 00:08:32.115
If you can hear it as well
as listen to it, you get some

00:08:32.115 --> 00:08:33.645
momentum into your reading.

00:08:33.745 --> 00:08:38.385
You get sort of a combined
effect, which is again, going to

00:08:38.385 --> 00:08:40.395
help you retain the vocabulary.

00:08:40.695 --> 00:08:46.035
And ultimately the goal is to acquire
enough vocabulary and familiarity with the

00:08:46.035 --> 00:08:48.165
language that you can read more and more.

00:08:48.195 --> 00:08:50.325
So it's a bit of a snowball situation.

00:08:50.565 --> 00:08:54.435
The more words you have, the more you
words you can learn, the more you're

00:08:54.435 --> 00:08:58.755
enjoying the process, because as you
acquire more words, you can deal with

00:08:58.875 --> 00:09:00.435
content that's of greater interest.

00:09:00.945 --> 00:09:05.025
And of course you can choose to
focus your activities so that,

00:09:05.535 --> 00:09:08.355
you know, what determines...

00:09:08.595 --> 00:09:11.085
I mentioned this system in the
UK, where they wanted to say,

00:09:11.085 --> 00:09:12.975
you must learn these 1700 words.

00:09:13.245 --> 00:09:19.079
Well, if my interest is in nuclear
physics, or it's in nature or it's

00:09:19.079 --> 00:09:25.530
in economics then I might actually
acquire and learn and remember some

00:09:25.530 --> 00:09:30.180
very low frequency words that are
relevant to my area of interest.

00:09:30.630 --> 00:09:35.930
And so we can actually control to some
extent what low frequency words we're

00:09:35.930 --> 00:09:39.930
going to encounter and make sure that
we encounter them over and over again.

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:44.069
And then every so often we can
change to another call it "domain"

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:47.670
where we again will be encountering
words somewhat more frequently.

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:53.250
We can have a strategy along those
lines, uh, at, at LingQ, we like to know

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:57.540
what percentage new words there are in
any content you want to import or any,

00:09:57.660 --> 00:10:00.300
anything you may find in our library.

00:10:00.565 --> 00:10:05.305
And typically for me, 15% unknown
words is kind of a sweet spot.

00:10:05.335 --> 00:10:11.095
I know that that if I were to spend
time and effort to listen to and read

00:10:11.125 --> 00:10:15.475
items with about 15% unknown words,
I'm going to be really improving,

00:10:15.475 --> 00:10:17.685
increasing, you know, creating more LingQ.

00:10:17.735 --> 00:10:21.475
As we say at LingQ, adding
to my vocabulary, improving

00:10:21.475 --> 00:10:25.465
my ability to understand more
and more demanding material.

00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:31.470
So, uh, the idea that sort of
comprehensive input, just go out

00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:35.520
and read and enjoy yourself, I think
is a little more complex than that.

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:41.640
Uh, I think if you rely solely on being
able to infer meaning, uh, without

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:44.970
looking words up in the dictionary,
without making that a little extra

00:10:44.970 --> 00:10:49.949
effort to learn the words, the amount of
material that you would have to consume

00:10:50.310 --> 00:10:51.960
would be hours and hours and hours.

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:57.015
And on the other hand, There are so many
words to learn that if you were to rely

00:10:57.015 --> 00:11:04.155
solely on say flashcard activities like
Anki, then you would deny yourself the

00:11:04.185 --> 00:11:08.295
pleasure of listening and reading the
whole familiarity with the language that

00:11:08.295 --> 00:11:10.275
comes through that kind of activity.

00:11:10.485 --> 00:11:12.975
So you need a bit of a
mixture in my opinion.

00:11:13.315 --> 00:11:17.790
Something between purely extensive
reading or on the other hand, deliberate

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:23.880
vocabulary study in order to make that
input, you know, push you forward.

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:28.140
And there's no question that if
you can achieve a high level of

00:11:28.170 --> 00:11:33.630
comprehension, both oral and written,
your speaking ability will come along.

00:11:33.930 --> 00:11:40.050
And all of that doesn't require you to
be, you know, totally accurate in your use

00:11:40.050 --> 00:11:42.329
of grammar that will improve over time.

00:11:43.290 --> 00:11:44.120
And shouldn't...

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:48.180
testing you on grammar, which is
often discouraging, shouldn't be

00:11:48.390 --> 00:11:52.740
sort of an obstacle to getting people
moving forward in the language.

00:11:53.070 --> 00:11:58.230
So comprehensible input as the
core, but sensible comprehensible

00:11:58.260 --> 00:12:04.260
input where modern tools are used to
help people progress more quickly.

00:12:04.500 --> 00:12:06.240
That's what I think is most effective.

00:12:06.630 --> 00:12:09.300
And, uh, I'll leave you a couple
of videos that I've done on

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:11.955
similar subjects in the past.

00:12:11.955 --> 00:12:16.845
And again, remember, please
subscribe to my videos and

00:12:16.845 --> 00:12:18.315
please send along your comments.

00:12:18.615 --> 00:12:19.505
Thank you for listening.

